DEEP[1]=('Chapter 6 is named Dhyāna Yoga or Ātma-Saṃyama Yoga, the yoga of meditation or self-control. It is the Gītā’s practical guide to meditation and closes the first group of six chapters, often called the karma ṣaṭka.',
 'The traditional sannyāsī gave up the sacred household fire and the Vedic rites. Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa does not reject formal renunciation; he says it is not enough. Śaṅkara accepts that karma yoga is called sannyāsa here as praise, because both share inner renunciation. Rāmānuja says the karma yogi is already a renunciant in the true sense. The chapter begins by placing meditation inside an active life.',
 'Before you meditate, check your work. A mind that clings to results will carry that clinging to the cushion.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 '<i>Saṅkalpa</i> has several meanings: resolve, intention, imagination, the mind’s planning for its own ends. Here it means the restless scheming that drives desire (4.19, 6.4, 6.24). Commentators note that the Gītā does not oppose good resolve; it opposes the endless selfish planning that keeps the mind agitated. Śaṅkara says renouncing saṅkalpa is the shared core of sannyāsa and yoga. The Yoga Sūtra speaks of calming the movements of the mind; letting go of saṅkalpa is a practical way into that calm.',
 'Notice how often your mind plans gains for yourself. Letting some of those plans rest is the first step to stillness.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 '<i>Ārurukṣu</i>, one who wishes to climb, and <i>yogārūḍha</i>, one who has climbed: two stages of practice. Commentators of every school use this verse as a map. Śaṅkara: action purifies the mind until one can climb; then renunciation and calm lead to knowledge. Rāmānuja: action leads to the vision of the self; once reached, calm sustains it. Many teachers compare it to a ladder: one climbs with effort and then stands at the top in stillness. The verse also guards against two errors: lazy calm before one is ready, and restless action after one has arrived.',
 'Ask honestly: am I still climbing, or have I arrived? Most of us need effort now. Ease will come.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'Commentators note two attachments: to objects of the senses and to actions themselves. The second is subtler. Many people let go of pleasures but cling to being busy, useful or in control. <i>Sarva-saṅkalpa-sannyāsī</i>, “one who has given up all scheming”, is the mark of arrival. Śaṅkara says this is the state where renunciation becomes natural. Rāmānuja says it is the state where the mind rests in the self. The verse gives a practical test: if we can sit still without craving objects or activity, we have climbed.',
 'Sit for five minutes with nothing to do. Notice whether the urge to be busy arises. That urge is also an attachment.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 'This is one of the most quoted verses in the Gītā. The word <i>ātman</i> is used six times, in different senses. Commentators explain: the higher self, our understanding and will, lifts the lower self, the restless mind. Śaṅkara says no one else is our real friend or enemy in the matter of liberation; it depends on us. Rāmānuja says the mind, rightly trained, is the self’s friend. Vivekananda loved this verse and often taught self-reliance from it. Modern psychology speaks of self-compassion and self-talk; the verse anticipates both.',
 'Speak to yourself today as a good friend would: honestly, kindly, with encouragement.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'This verse explains the last: whether the self is friend or enemy depends on whether it has been mastered. Commentators note the military language: <i>jita</i>, conquered; <i>śatru</i>, enemy. Arjuna is a warrior; Kṛṣṇa speaks his language. Śaṅkara says the unconquered mind harms us like a real enemy, driving us into wrong action. Rāmānuja says the same mind, trained by yoga, becomes our closest ally. The Dhammapada (verse 160) has a similar thought: the self is the master of the self; who else could be the master?',
 'Your mind can be your toughest critic or your best coach. Training it is the most useful work you can do.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 'Commentators read <i>paramātmā samāhitaḥ</i> in two ways. Some take <i>paramātmā</i> as the Supreme Self, on which the yogi’s mind is fixed. Others take it as the yogi’s own self, now elevated and steady. Śaṅkara takes it as the Self of the knower, which, freed of agitation, becomes the Supreme. Rāmānuja reads it as the yogi’s self in its supreme state. The verse widens the pairs of opposites from physical (cold and heat) to social (honour and dishonour), which many find harder to bear. Chapter 12 (12.18–19) returns to praise and blame.',
 'Praise and criticism are like weather. Notice them, learn what is useful, and let the rest blow over.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 '<i>Kūṭastha</i>, literally “standing on the peak” or “like an anvil”, describes something that stays unchanged while things happen to it. The word returns in 12.3 and 15.16. Commentators note that seeing gold and clay as equal is not ignorance of value but freedom from greed. The sage knows gold can buy things but does not crave it. Śaṅkara says the knower sees the one Self in all, so value differences do not disturb him. Rāmānuja says the knower sees all matter as matter, different from the self. Ramakrishna famously practised by holding a coin in one hand and a clod in the other, saying “money is earth”, and throwing both into the Ganges.',
 'Money is useful; it is not the measure of a person or a life. Hold it lightly.')
DEEP[9]=('<i>Suhṛn-mitrāry-udāsīna-madhyastha-dveṣya-bandhuṣu</i>, a long compound listing well-wishers, friends, enemies, the indifferent, the neutral, the hateful and relatives.',
 'Commentators distinguish the terms carefully. A <i>suhṛt</i> helps without expecting return. A <i>mitra</i> is a friend through affection. An <i>ari</i> is an enemy. The <i>udāsīna</i> is indifferent to both sides, and the <i>madhyastha</i> is an impartial mediator. The <i>dveṣya</i> is one who deserves dislike. The <i>bandhu</i> is a relative. The verse is the hardest test of equanimity so far, after objects in 6.8. Śaṅkara says equal-mindedness means no hatred or attachment, while still acting appropriately. Rāmānuja says the yogi sees the same self in all. For Arjuna, facing kinsmen as enemies, the verse is pointed.',
 'Equal-mindedness does not mean treating everyone identically. It means carrying no hatred and no favouritism in the heart.')
