 '<i>Śanaiḥ śanaiḥ</i>, “slowly, slowly”, is beloved by meditators. Commentators note that the Gītā never promises sudden results; it asks for patient, gradual practice. <i>Dhṛti</i>, firmness or steady will, is explained in 18.33–35. Śaṅkara says the mind should be fixed on the Self, with no other thought. Rāmānuja says the mind should rest on the self, thinking of nothing else. Modern teachers often add that “thinking nothing” is not forcing thoughts away, but letting them settle as attention rests elsewhere.',
