 'This verse describes the heart of every meditation practice. Commentators note the repetition <i>yato yato</i> and <i>tatas tato</i>, “from wherever, from there”: the mind will wander many times, and each time it is brought back. Śaṅkara says one should see the faults of the objects the mind runs to and bring it back by dispassion. Rāmānuja says one should see the self’s greater joy and bring the mind back to it. Modern mindfulness teachers say the same: the wandering is not failure; the noticing and returning is the practice.',
