 'This verse closely echoes the Īśā Upaniṣad (6): <i>yas tu sarvāṇi bhūtāny ātmany evānupaśyati, sarva-bhūteṣu cātmānaṃ tato na vijugupsate</i>, “one who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings hates none”. Commentators of every school treasure it. Śaṅkara: the one Self in all. Rāmānuja: all selves alike in nature, since each is pure knowledge. Madhva: the Lord present in all. The fruit of meditation is not withdrawal from the world but a new way of seeing it. The vision leads straight to compassion (6.32).',
